// DISTRIBUTED textbook-scatter schedule (TASK-0432) — the multi-worker
// "broaden" of the single-worker `textbook.sched.nuc` (TASK-0430) and the
// call-wrapped-index sibling of `distributed.scatter.sched.nuc`
// (TASK-0384).
//
// Drives `prog.textbook.algo.nuc` (the X1' textbook scatter where the bin
// is COMPUTED by a PURE kernel call in INDEX position:
// `histogram[bucket(input[i])] <-- inc(histogram[bucket(input[i])])`).
// The e2e harness selects the algorithm from this `schedule for` path
// (TASK-0049.03) and pairs `kernels.textbook.rs` by the variant rule
// (TASK-0049.08), so an `08-histogram/distributed.textbook` cell compiles
// the textbook variant under the distributed placement.
//
// The shape (identical to distributed.scatter, with `bucket` added on the
// compute workers):
//   host    — owns I/O (load_input / save_output) and the cross-worker
//             partial-histogram element-wise-sum combine.
//   w0..w3  — each compute worker owns a DISJOINT i-band of `input`
//             (the outer `i` loop is the partition axis), COMPUTES each
//             element's bin LOCALLY via `bucket(input[i])`, and scatters
//             into its OWN PRIVATE full-width partial histogram
//             (`let mut histogram: Vec<i32> = vec![0; 16]`).
//
// Why this is sound (the same boundary as TASK-0384). The partition is
// over the INPUT INDEX `i`, NOT over histogram bins. Each worker holds
// the FULL `histogram` (whole-array replicate, because its write index
// `bucket(input[i])` is data-dependent → opaque dim in
// `transfer_inject::record_access_per_dim` → whole-array broadcast), so a
// scatter to ANY bin lands in that worker's private partial. The host
// then sums the four partials ELEMENT-WISE (TASK-0343
// `collect_accumulate_waits` + `render_wait_assign(accumulate)`:
// `histogram[k] = histogram[k].wrapping_add(partial_w[k])`). That sum
// equals the single-worker histogram exactly: every input element is
// counted once, in exactly one worker's band, into the bin its `bucket`
// selects.
//
// Why the `bucket()` wrapper does NOT change the accumulate class. The
// self-read `histogram[bucket(input[i])]` (inside `inc(...)`) and the LHS
// `histogram[bucket(input[i])]` carry the IDENTICAL `bucket(input[i])`
// `IrExpr::Call`, so the cumulative-array discriminator
// (`sidecar::collect_cumulative_data_names`, which keys on the self-read
// index STRUCTURALLY DIFFERING from the LHS index) does NOT fire ⇒ NOT
// cumulative ⇒ stays a `wrapping_add` ACCUMULATE fan-in, the same class
// as `distributed.scatter`. This is the first test of that claim on the
// actual distributed / transfer-inject codegen path (it was
// forward-verification only for the single-worker cell).
//
// `bucket` is placed on the compute workers (NOT host): it is a pure
// index-computing kernel called inside the partitioned loop body, so each
// worker evaluates it locally on its own i-band — no `bucket` value
// crosses a channel.
//
// A BIN partition (a worker owning only a band of `histogram`) would be
// UNSOUND for this replicate-then-sum shape (a worker would silently drop
// every scatter that lands outside its band). The compiler keeps that
// shape FATAL via the halo `is_scatter_rmw` gate
// (`error_is_fatal_under_partition` →
// `scatter_target_replicates_whole_array`): a scatter RMW under partition
// is admitted ONLY when no partitioned iv affinely indexes the scatter
// target. Here the only iv `i` indexes the SOURCE `input[i]`, so
// `histogram` is never affinely banded — the admit shape.
//
// Cross-worker edges:
//   input      host -> { w0..w3 }   (host loads, each worker consumes
//                                    its i-band)
//   histogram  { w0..w3 } -> host   (workers produce full-width
//                                    partials, host element-wise-sums)
//
// All `sync` transfers. `input` is the only host->worker broadcast and
// there is no gather-index array (the bin is computed LOCALLY per worker
// from its own `input` band), so the Push/Wait FIFO order trap
// (project-mp-tcp-event-vs-bufsync-safety-profile / TASK-0389) does NOT
// bite on the strict-FIFO backends — the same FIFO profile as
// distributed.scatter. Output is bit-identical to 08-histogram/naive,
// /scatter, /distributed.scatter, and reference.bin (verified, TASK-0432).

schedule for "../prog.textbook.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // I/O + cross-worker combine on host.
    place load_input  on host;
    place save_output on host;

    // The pure bin-bucketing and per-hit increment kernels distributed
    // across the compute workers — both evaluated locally on each
    // worker's i-band, no cross-worker value crossing.
    place bucket on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };
    place inc    on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // Outer loop is the partition axis; one i-band per compute worker.
    loop i : partition=workers;

    // Cross-worker data — one transfer per data symbol that crosses.
    // `input` i-bands to each worker; `histogram` replicates whole-array
    // (data-dependent write index ⇒ opaque dim ⇒ whole-array broadcast)
    // and the partials are element-wise-summed at the host.
    transfer input     : sync;
    transfer histogram : sync;
}
